        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Build Generative UI Apps with A2UI & CopilotKit Agents]]></title>
            <description><![CDATA[See Google A2Ui in action! Learn how to natively render agentic UIs across Langchain, AWS Strands, Microsoft agents, and Google ADK using CopilotKit SDKs for A2UI.]]></description>
            <link>https://developers.googleblog.com/build-generative-ui-apps-with-a2ui-copilotkit-agents/</link>
            <guid isPermaLink="false">https://developers.googleblog.com/build-generative-ui-apps-with-a2ui-copilotkit-agents/</guid>
            <pubDate>Thu, 13 Feb 2020 21:56:00 GMT</pubDate>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Hill-Climbing Agent Quality with Agents CLI Evaluation]]></title>
            <description><![CDATA[This post walks through the Agents CLI evaluation loop for systematically improving agent performance through continuous testing and grading. It demonstrates how to catch prompt regressions, evaluate model backend swaps using side-by-side trace comparisons, and automate evaluation passes. Developers will also learn how to integrate nightly scheduled runs with threshold-based alerting to track quality over time.]]></description>
            <link>https://developers.googleblog.com/hill-climbing-agent-quality-with-agents-cli-evaluation/</link>
            <guid isPermaLink="false">https://developers.googleblog.com/hill-climbing-agent-quality-with-agents-cli-evaluation/</guid>
            <pubDate>Thu, 16 Jan 2020 00:07:00 GMT</pubDate>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Smaller apps and faster GPU inference with LiteRT in Google Play services]]></title>
            <description><![CDATA[LiteRT in Google Play services now offers GPU inference powered by ML Drift, delivering about 1.4x faster performance alongside the new CompiledModel API. Switching from bundled binaries reduces app size by approximately 8 MB while enabling seamless, automatic runtime updates across billions of devices. Developers can easily adopt these updates by updating their dependencies and utilizing the new Android Studio LiteRT skill for automated migration.]]></description>
            <link>https://developers.googleblog.com/smaller-apps-and-faster-gpu-inference-with-litert-in-google-play-services/</link>
            <guid isPermaLink="false">https://developers.googleblog.com/smaller-apps-and-faster-gpu-inference-with-litert-in-google-play-services/</guid>
            <pubDate>Thu, 13 Feb 2020 19:38:00 GMT</pubDate>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Building Conversational Commerce with Multi-Agent Swarms on GCP: A Working Prototype]]></title>
            <description><![CDATA[This document provides a publication template and metadata structure for a technical blog post focused on building Conversational User Interfaces (CUIs) using AI. It outlines key developer focus areas, product stacks, and approval workflows required for publishing technical content through Google Developer Studio. The framework helps engineers organize their guides and solutions to effectively share AI-driven conversational interface designs with the developer community.]]></description>
            <link>https://developers.googleblog.com/building-conversational-commerce-with-multi-agent-swarms-on-gcp-a-working-prototype/</link>
            <guid isPermaLink="false">https://developers.googleblog.com/building-conversational-commerce-with-multi-agent-swarms-on-gcp-a-working-prototype/</guid>
            <pubDate>Sat, 13 Jun 2020 05:32:00 GMT</pubDate>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Fast, reliable A2UI, with swappable inference formats]]></title>
            <description><![CDATA[A2UI now supports swappable inference formats, introducing A2UI Express which uses up to 3x fewer tokens and cuts latency by compiling compact Python-style syntax into standard A2UI JSON via Agent SDKs. Developers can also use controlled generation with TypeSafe's Jev system-decision models to render known-shape screens in under 300 ms while smaller models stream text. These updates require no changes to frontend renderers or component catalogs, offering flexible performance levers for generative UI development across Python, TypeScript, and Dart.]]></description>
            <link>https://developers.googleblog.com/fast-reliable-a2ui/</link>
            <guid isPermaLink="false">https://developers.googleblog.com/fast-reliable-a2ui/</guid>
            <pubDate>Wed, 23 Dec 2020 03:31:00 GMT</pubDate>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Architecting Governed, Self-Evolving AI Agents on Google Cloud]]></title>
            <description><![CDATA[Learn how Project Chrysalis combines Google Cloud's Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform, Agent Development Kit (ADK), BigQuery, and a four-gate defense-in-depth security stack (Model Armor, Cloud KMS, Binary Authorization, and OPA) to build governed, self-evolving AI agents that remediate live production outages in 1.8 seconds.]]></description>
            <link>https://developers.googleblog.com/architecting-governed-self-evolving-ai-agents-on-gemini-agent-platform/</link>
            <guid isPermaLink="false">https://developers.googleblog.com/architecting-governed-self-evolving-ai-agents-on-gemini-agent-platform/</guid>
            <pubDate>Thu, 05 Nov 2020 11:20:00 GMT</pubDate>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Powering the Agent Development Lifecycle with any framework, from ADK to LangChain and beyond]]></title>
            <description><![CDATA[The Agents CLI Extension System enables developers to use frameworks like LangChain, LangGraph, or custom enterprise stacks while retaining a unified deployment and evaluation workflow. It details a first-party LangChain template that scaffolds, runs, and deploys agents without requiring the Agent Development Kit (ADK). Additionally, it demonstrates how platform teams can author custom extensions to enforce policies such as automated software bill of materials (SBOM) scans during deployment.]]></description>
            <link>https://developers.googleblog.com/powering-the-agent-development-lifecycle-with-any-framework-from-adk-to-langchain-and-beyond/</link>
            <guid isPermaLink="false">https://developers.googleblog.com/powering-the-agent-development-lifecycle-with-any-framework-from-adk-to-langchain-and-beyond/</guid>
            <pubDate>Fri, 27 Mar 2020 13:07:00 GMT</pubDate>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Bringing Visual Graph Debugging to Custom Edge Compilers with Model Explorer]]></title>
            <description><![CDATA[Google AI Edge Model Explorer now supports custom compiler intermediate representations and open-source adapters like TOSA, VGF, and PTE for on-device machine learning. Developers can leverage hierarchical graph visualization, split-pane views, and per-node trace overlays to catch export regressions and verify hardware delegation. These tools enable engineering teams to transparently debug execution graphs and pinpoint cycle-level latency bottlenecks locally.]]></description>
            <link>https://developers.googleblog.com/bringing-visual-graph-debugging-to-custom-edge-compilers-with-model-explorer/</link>
            <guid isPermaLink="false">https://developers.googleblog.com/bringing-visual-graph-debugging-to-custom-edge-compilers-with-model-explorer/</guid>
            <pubDate>Sat, 24 Oct 2020 22:13:00 GMT</pubDate>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Integrating TorchTPU into TorchTitan and Optimizing Models for TPU]]></title>
            <description><![CDATA[TorchTPU integrates a native torch.device("tpu") backend directly into PyTorch, allowing developers to train large-scale models on Google Cloud TPUs without rewriting code in JAX. The TorchTitan TPU fork enhances this with production-grade scaling primitives like SimpleFSDP, Graph Trainer, and custom Pallas kernels that achieve high Model Flops Utilization (MFU) across dense and MoE architectures. Developers can leverage these battle-tested optimization playbooks, recipes, and the Hugging Face modeling backend to efficiently scale their own workloads on TPU infrastructure.]]></description>
            <link>https://developers.googleblog.com/integrating-torchtpu-into-torchtitan-and-optimizing-models-for-tpu/</link>
            <guid isPermaLink="false">https://developers.googleblog.com/integrating-torchtpu-into-torchtitan-and-optimizing-models-for-tpu/</guid>
            <pubDate>Wed, 10 Jun 2020 12:38:00 GMT</pubDate>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Announcing New vLLM and SGLang Inference Engines on TPU]]></title>
            <description><![CDATA[This post announces native PyTorch backends for vLLM and SGLang powered by TorchTPU, enabling developers to run and optimize serving workloads using familiar PyTorch tools. Built in collaboration with open-source project maintainers, these engines include optimized TPU kernel implementations, production-ready features like KV cache management with TPU Sync, and disaggregated serving. Public benchmarks demonstrate state-of-the-art performance and industry-leading cost efficiency on Ironwood TPU across long-context and agentic workloads.]]></description>
            <link>https://developers.googleblog.com/announcing-new-vllm-and-sglang-inference-engines-on-tpu/</link>
            <guid isPermaLink="false">https://developers.googleblog.com/announcing-new-vllm-and-sglang-inference-engines-on-tpu/</guid>
            <pubDate>Tue, 21 Apr 2020 17:56:00 GMT</pubDate>
        </item>
